+# Decision: Whole-Visit Revisions for Concurrent Visit Edits
+
+## Context
+
+TidePlot collects offline visit records from 12 shared tablets. A visit contains a plot reference, observation date, observer names, species cover estimates, and notes. Workers sometimes correct the same visit independently after comparing field notes, and a correction can arrive days after the revision it started from was fetched. The record store must retain every submitted edit, produce a current visit for review, and give review-export a defensible basis for deciding which visits enter the partner's Friday CSV.
+
+Two concurrency policies are credible. This decision selects one and records why the other remains a live alternative. The choice determines the submission envelope tablet-sync must retain and send, the review work review-export must present, and which visit states can enter an export. Getting it wrong in one direction silently combines scientific edits no coordinator notices; getting it wrong in the other creates a review queue two coordinators cannot clear before the weekly export.
+
+The governing constraints are the HLD tenets: do not silently discard a submitted edit; keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable; and prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator. The HLD also treats the server as unable to infer which fields are scientifically related.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+No gate eliminates an option. Both policies can retain original submissions and both can be made inspectable, so the choice turns on weighted criteria rather than a pass/fail boundary.
+
+| Criterion | Importance | Basis |
+|---|---|---|
+| Preserves the submitted visit as the unit of scientific judgment | Major | A visit's fields are observed together; a coordinator evaluates a complete observation, not an arbitrary field subset. Ties to the tenet *Do not silently discard a submitted edit*. |
+| Risk of unnoticed auto-combination across related scientific edits | Major | The server cannot infer that a species identification and its cover estimate are related, or that an observer change and a note are related. Ties to the goal *Make reviewable problems visible before the Friday export*. |
+| Review workload two coordinators can clear | Major | Two coordinators handle questions about submitted observations; a Friday export deadline bounds the queue. Ties to the tenet *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*. |
+| Maintenance burden of combination rules | Major | Field-aware merging needs maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Ties to the two-coordinator tenet. |
+| Explainability of the exported value | Major | A coordinator or partner must be able to trace an exported value to submitted observations. Ties to the tenet *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*. |
+| Implementation complexity in the record store | Moderate | The store owns revision identity, conflict detection, review state, and resolution. |
+| Tablet storage and submission size | Moderate | Tablets can be offline for 48 hours with 12 devices and about 6,000 visits per season; a policy that requires base snapshots or full payloads changes local storage and upload size. |
+| Review-queue volume versus review-item complexity | Moderate | A policy can produce many simple comparisons or fewer but more intricate field-level reviews. |
+| Ability to reject all competing changes | Moderate | A coordinator may need to retain the state that predates an episode when no submitted correction is acceptable. |
+| Cost of adding a new field or list element | Minor | The visit schema may gain fields; a policy that requires per-field merge rules pays a repeated cost. |
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-Visit Revisions
+
+Each accepted submission stores the complete visit payload as one immutable revision and names the revision it started from. The first revision becomes current. A submission based on the current revision becomes the new current revision. A submission based on any other revision opens a review episode: the previous current revision becomes the baseline, the new revision becomes a competing revision, and the visit has no current revision until a coordinator resolves the episode. Every revision accepted during the episode joins the candidate set, and the coordinator selects one candidate as the new current revision. Unresolved visits are held out of the export. Tablets retain the base revision ID for each draft and submit the complete visit; review-export compares complete revisions and exposes the resolution record.
+
+**Demonstration.** Worker A corrects an observer name; worker B adds a missing species; both began from revision R7. A submits first and becomes the current revision, R8. B's submission names R7 as its base, so it opens an episode with R8 as the baseline and B's complete revision as the competing revision. The visit is unresolved until a coordinator compares R8 with B's revision and selects one. If B's revision is selected, A's R8 remains in history as a non-selected candidate.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- Preservation of the submitted visit as the unit of scientific judgment: **strong** — each revision is a complete observed visit; review compares whole visits rather than field fragments.
+- Risk of unnoticed auto-combination: **strong** — the store never combines fields, so a related pair of edits cannot be merged without review.
+- Review workload: **weak** — even unrelated edits such as an observer correction and a new species create competing revisions and a review item.
+- Maintenance burden of combination rules: **strong** — there are no field-level merge rules to write or maintain.
+- Explainability: **strong** — the exported value is a selected complete revision, and the resolution record names the baseline, competing revisions, coordinator, and selection.
+- Implementation complexity: **partial** — revision trees, episodes, candidate selection, idempotency, and stale-resolution checks are needed, but no merge-rule engine.
+- Tablet storage and submission size: **partial** — tablets retain a base revision ID, but every submission carries the complete visit payload; base snapshots are unnecessary.
+- Review-queue volume versus item complexity: **weak on volume, strong on simplicity** — the queue is larger, but each item is a complete-visit comparison.
+- Ability to reject all competing changes: **strong** — the baseline is a candidate, so a coordinator can select the pre-episode state.
+- Cost of adding a new field or list element: **strong** — a new field travels in the complete payload without a new merge rule.
+
+**Summary.** Trades a larger review queue for whole-visit fidelity, explainability, and no merge-rule maintenance.
+
+### Field-Aware Three-Way Merge
+
+Each submission supplies the base state the tablet started from and a structured set of changes. The server combines changes that touch disjoint fields and sends only overlapping changes to review. Maintained rules decide how each field type combines: scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries each need their own rule. The tablet retains a base snapshot and sends structured changes; review-export shows field-level provenance for automatically combined fields and a review view for overlaps. Original submissions are retained alongside the combined current state.
+
+**Demonstration.** Worker A corrects an observer name; worker B adds a missing species; both began from the same base. The observer field and the species-entry list are disjoint under the maintained rules, so the server combines them automatically. No coordinator review is required. If both workers change the same species' cover estimate, that field overlaps and the visit enters review with both values and their provenance.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- Preservation of the submitted visit as the unit of scientific judgment: **weak** — the exported record is an assembly of fields from different submissions, not either worker's observed visit as a whole.
+- Risk of unnoticed auto-combination: **weak** — rules match field structure and names, not scientific relationships; an automatically combined observer change and species revision can be scientifically inconsistent without triggering overlap review.
+- Review workload: **strong** — disjoint edits combine automatically, so the coordinator queue is smaller and more visits reach export.
+- Maintenance burden of combination rules: **weak** — rules must be written, tested, and maintained for every scalar, observer-list, and species-entry case, including future fields.
+- Explainability: **partial** — field-level provenance can explain each exported field, but the combined visit has no single submitted counterpart and the provenance view is more intricate than a revision comparison.
+- Implementation complexity: **weak** — the store needs a merge engine, per-type rules, overlap detection, and provenance storage in addition to revision retention.
+- Tablet storage and submission size: **weak** — tablets retain base snapshots and send structured changes rather than a complete visit; submissions may be smaller, but local storage grows with retained bases.
+- Review-queue volume versus item complexity: **strong on volume, weak on complexity** — fewer reviews, but each overlap review is a field-level merge judgment.
+- Ability to reject all competing changes: **partial** — the base state is available, but an automatically combined current state may already contain parts of several submissions, so rejecting all changes is less direct than selecting a baseline revision.
+- Cost of adding a new field or list element: **weak** — each new field type needs a combination rule and overlap semantics.
+
+**Summary.** Trades merge-rule maintenance and semantic-combination risk for a smaller review queue and more visits eligible for export.
+
+## Selection
+
+Whole-visit revisions is selected. The decisive criteria are the risk of unnoticed auto-combination and preservation of the submitted visit as the unit of scientific judgment. The team's exposure is asymmetric: an auto-combined species identification and cover estimate, or an observer change combined with a related note, can produce a plausible record that no coordinator sees. Whole-visit revisions never combine fields, so a related pair of edits cannot bypass review. The cost is the review workload: unrelated edits compete too, and unresolved visits stay out of the export. The team accepts that larger queue rather than maintaining merge rules that can combine scientifically related edits without noticing.
+
+The decision turns on the tenet *Do not silently discard a submitted edit* — every revision is retained and review decides, never an automatic field combination — and on *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable* — the exported value is a selected complete revision with a resolution record. It also serves *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*: there are no merge rules for the coordinators or a maintainer to keep correct. It works against the review-workload criterion, and that cost is accepted deliberately.
+
+Field-aware three-way merge remains a credible alternative rather than a rejected design. It is the better choice if the coordinator queue proves unmanageable at the team's actual correction rate, or if the team can constrain merge rules to a small, audited set whose scientific relationships are known. It is also the better choice if submissions must shrink or if field-level provenance becomes a partner requirement. Reopening this decision would require changing the tablet contract to retain base snapshots and send structured changes, and changing review-export to present field-level provenance; those capabilities are not built now.
+
+### Implications and Downstream Obligations
+
+Whole-visit revisions foreclose automatic combination of disjoint edits and field-level provenance as the mechanism for producing a current visit. They obligate the following:
+
+- **record-store**: retain immutable complete-visit revisions with base revision IDs; treat the submitted visit as the review unit; open a review episode on divergence; expose the baseline and competing revisions; record coordinator selection, non-selected candidates, and the resolution; provide idempotent submission and resolution, submission history, current-state retrieval, and a per-visit state version.
+- **tablet-sync**: retain each draft's base revision ID; submit complete visits rather than field diffs; carry an idempotency key; treat a submission as not necessarily becoming current; display pending competing revisions and unresolved status; explain a later resolution and why a submission was not selected. Local storage needs drafts and base IDs, not retained base snapshots.
+- **review-export**: provide a revision comparison screen over complete visit payloads, including revision lineage, authors, receipt times, the episode baseline, and competing revisions; support selecting one candidate; hold unresolved visits out of the CSV; use the record store's state version to detect a state change before export; include the resolution provenance in the exported record's explanation.
+- **site-catalog**: no change to plot identity or snapshot distribution; visit revisions continue to reference stable plot UUIDs.
