+## Concurrency Policy: Whole-Visit Revisions
+
+Each accepted submission stores the complete visit payload as one revision and names the revision it started from. A revision's **base revision** is the revision supplied by the submitting tablet; the first revision for a visit has no base. Revisions are immutable and are never overwritten or removed by a later submission.
+
+### Revision Set and Conflict Detection
+
+For each visit, the store maintains:
+
+- the set of accepted revisions, linked by base revision;
+- the **current revision**, the accepted revision that represents the visit while the visit is resolved;
+- at most one open **review episode**, created when an edit diverges from the current revision;
+- a **state version**, incremented on every accepted submission or resolution.
+
+A visit is `resolved` when it has a current revision and no open review episode. It is `unresolved` when an open review episode exists.
+
+When a submission is accepted:
+
+- If the visit has no revisions, the new revision becomes the current revision and the visit is resolved.
+- If the visit is resolved and the submission's base revision equals the current revision, the new revision becomes the current revision and the visit remains resolved. This is the ordinary successive-edit path.
+- Otherwise, the visit becomes unresolved. If no review episode is open, one opens with the previous current revision as its **baseline revision**. The new revision is a **competing revision**. The visit has no current revision while the episode remains open.
+- If a review episode is already open, the new revision joins that episode as another competing revision. It may name any known revision of the visit as its base, including the baseline, another competing revision, or a revision from an earlier episode.
+
+A submission may name no base revision. When the visit already has revisions, such a revision is accepted as a competing revision in the open or newly opened episode; it is not rejected merely because its starting state is unknown.
+
+Divergence is defined by base identity, not payload equality. A submission whose base is not the current revision is competing even when its payload is identical to the current revision. The store performs no content-based deduplication of revisions.
+
+Receipt order determines which revision becomes current before a conflicting revision arrives. It does not decide the outcome of a review episode: every revision accepted while an episode is open remains a candidate for the coordinator, and a later conflicting revision always opens or joins an episode rather than being discarded.
+
+### Review Episode
+
+An open episode records:
+
+- its baseline revision, the current revision at the moment divergence began;
+- every competing revision accepted before resolution;
+- the candidate set available to the coordinator: the baseline revision plus every competing revision.
+
+The candidate set preserves the complete visits a coordinator may choose between. A competing revision based on another competing revision remains a separate candidate; selecting it selects the content it contains, including its ancestor's content.
+
+Resolution selects one candidate and closes the episode. Every other candidate is recorded as not selected in that episode. Non-selection is scoped to the episode: a non-selected revision remains in submission history and may be named as the base of a later submission. Such a submission creates a new revision in a later episode; the earlier resolution record is not changed.
+
+### Resolution
+
+A coordinator resolves an open episode by selecting one candidate revision as the visit's current revision. Selecting the baseline revision rejects every competing revision in the episode; selecting a competing revision replaces the baseline with that revision's complete visit. Every revision in the episode remains in submission history after resolution.
+
+A resolution records the coordinator identity, the selected revision, the full candidate set that contributed to the review, the non-selected candidate revisions, the episode ID, and the resolution time. Resolution does not author a new visit. If a coordinator needs a corrected visit that combines or edits the candidates, that correction is submitted afterward through the ordinary submission path and becomes a normal successor to the newly current revision.
+
+A resolution request names the review episode it resolves and the visit state version the coordinator reviewed. If the episode has gained a competing revision or the visit state version has changed since that version, the request is rejected as stale; the coordinator retrieves the current episode and resolves again. This prevents a resolution from closing work the coordinator did not see.
+
+A resolution request carries an idempotency key scoped to the coordinator. A retry with the same key and an identical request returns the original successful resolution outcome; reuse with a different request is rejected. Failed resolution requests are not recorded for idempotent replay and may be retried. Resolving a visit with no open episode is rejected.
+
+### Visit States
+
+| Status | Meaning | Current revision | Open episode |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `resolved` | The visit has an accepted state and no competing revisions awaiting review | Set | None |
+| `unresolved` | At least one competing revision awaits review | None | Baseline plus competing revisions |
+
+An unresolved visit has no current revision. Consumers must not treat the baseline or any competing revision as the visit's accepted state while the episode is open.
 
 ## Submission and Retrieval
 
-Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved or combined representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
+### Submission Envelope
+
+A submission contains:
+
+| Field | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| visit ID | Client-generated UUID of the visit; supplied in the request path |
+| base revision ID | Revision the tablet edited, or absent when the tablet has no starting revision |
+| idempotency key | Client-generated key, unique within the submitting worker's submissions across all visits |
+| payload | Complete visit fields: plot ID, observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, and notes |
+
+The author identity comes from authentication; the server assigns a globally unique revision ID and receipt time.
+
+The store authenticates the submitting worker and validates field types and plot references before accepting a submission. A base revision, when present, must belong to the same visit. A retry with the same idempotency key and identical request (visit, base revision, and payload) returns the original acceptance result without creating another revision; reuse of the key with a different request is rejected and leaves the original revision unchanged. Payload equality is evaluated on the validated, canonically serialized payload: field order is irrelevant, while array order and duplicate array entries are significant. Rejected submissions do not consume their idempotency keys, so a worker may correct a bad payload and retry with the same key.
+
+Accepted submissions return the revision ID and original receipt time, the visit's current status, the visit state version, the current revision ID when resolved, and the review episode ID when unresolved. On an idempotent retry, the revision ID and receipt time are the original ones; status, state version, and current revision reflect the visit at the time of the retry response.
+
+### Current-State Retrieval
+
+Current-state retrieval returns:
+
+- the visit ID, status, and state version;
+- when resolved, the current revision with its payload, author, and receipt time;
+- when unresolved, the review episode ID, the baseline revision ID, and each competing revision's ID, base revision ID, author, and receipt time.
+
+Full competing payloads are available from submission-history retrieval rather than duplicated in the current-state response. A visit ID with no accepted revisions is unknown and returns not found.
+
+### Submission-History Retrieval
+
+Submission-history retrieval returns every revision for the visit with its payload, base revision ID, author, receipt time, and review episode membership. Revisions are ordered by server receipt time, with revision ID as the tie-breaker; revision IDs are globally unique identifiers and carry no ordering guarantee. The response also returns the visit's resolution records, including each episode's selected revision and non-selected candidate revisions. The history is the source for revision comparison and for explaining how the current state was reached. Revisions from resolved earlier episodes remain in the history.
+
+### Resolution Operation
+
+Resolution requires a coordinator authorization and is available only while the visit is unresolved. The request names the review episode, the expected visit state version, and the selected candidate revision, and may include a note. The selected revision must be the episode's baseline or one of its competing revisions. On success the visit becomes resolved with the selected revision as current, the episode closes, the state version increments, and the resolution record is retained with the revision history.
+
+A resolution idempotency key is unique within the coordinator's resolution requests across all visits. It is a separate namespace from submission idempotency keys.
+
+## Export Eligibility Signal
+
+The record store publishes each visit's status. A visit is eligible to enter review-export's reviewed CSV only while its status is `resolved`; review-export holds `unresolved` visits out of the export set. Review-export may apply additional review rules in its own node.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
 
-Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. Exactly how those states are represented remains part of the open choice.
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Concurrent visit edits | Whole-visit revisions: each submission is a complete visit revision naming its base; divergent revisions go to review (see [the decision doc](decisions/whole-visit-revisions.md)) | Field-aware three-way merge of disjoint field changes | Whole-visit revisions preserve the worker's submitted visit as a unit and need no field-level merge rules. The team accepts a larger review queue in exchange for not auto-combining edits whose scientific relationships the server cannot infer. |
+| What a review episode exposes | Baseline revision plus every revision accepted before resolution | Only the current open branch tips; only revisions sharing one common base | The baseline lets a coordinator reject all competing changes. Keeping every revision accepted during the episode preserves submissions that arrived while the visit was already unresolved, including revisions built on another competing revision. |
+| Coordinators' write path during review | Resolution selects an existing candidate; corrections are submitted afterward as ordinary revisions | Coordinator authors a new merged or corrected revision inside the resolution operation | Selection keeps resolution a comparison-and-choice operation with a simple audit record. A coordinator who needs a combined visit can submit one through the same path as any worker, preserving whole-visit revision history. |
+| Current state of an unresolved visit | No current revision while an episode is open | Keep the baseline available as current with an unresolved flag | Withholding a current revision prevents an accidental export or downstream use of a state that is under review. The baseline remains explicitly identified for comparison. |
 
